   "source": [
    "# Quiz: Visualizing Relationships: All About Honey 🍯\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-12-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Use this library to visualize relationships:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Seaborn</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Seaborn is a good library for visualizing relationships between variables.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Merborn</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The library for showing relationships is Seaborn.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Reborn</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The library for showing relationships is Seaborn.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-12-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Use Seaborn's <c>relplot</c> to visualize</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>categorical relationships</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. relplot can show categorical data, but its purpose is to show statistical relationships.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>statistical relationships</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. relplot draws scatter and line plots that show statistical relationships between variables.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>special relationships</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. relplot shows statistical relationships, which help you see how variables relate to each other.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-12-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>By editing a scatterplot's hue, you can:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>display a dataset's distribution</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Hue colors each point by a value, like year, so you can see how the data changes across it.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>display item colors</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Hue does use color, but to show a value in the data, like year, not the items' own colors.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>display temperatures</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Hue colors each point by a value in the data, like year in the honey data.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-12-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A more accessible way to display distribution is:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Using datapoint shape variation</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Using datapoint size variation</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Using either of the above</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-12-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Using Seaborn's <c>relplot</c> you can view an aggregation of data around a line chart</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>true</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A relplot line chart shows the mean and a 95% confidence interval at each x value.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>false</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. relplot's line chart aggregates the data, showing the mean and a confidence interval around it.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-12-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Facet grids help visualize</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a given facet of your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A facet grid takes one facet of your data, like year, and makes a plot for each one.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>outliers in your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A facet grid makes one plot per facet, like year, for easy side-by-side comparison.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>progression in your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A facet grid splits the data by one facet, such as year, and draws a plot for each.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
